<description>
Fill rectangular cavities in a template shape by placing source sprites into matching holes.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. A dominant "base" color forms a large frame/template with rectangular gaps.
- Small colored sprites sit outside the template region.

Rule:
- The base color (largest connected component excluding bg) defines the template bbox.
- Each rectangular hole inside the template matches exactly one outside sprite by size.
- Place each sprite into its matching cavity; output is the filled template bbox.

Key insight: Sprite bounding boxes match cavity dimensions exactly — it's a jigsaw-fit puzzle where each piece has a unique slot.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "source sprites s outside the template bbox",
  "dst": "rectangular cavities d inside the template bbox",
  "match_cond": "s.bbox == d.rect (d is an all-hole rectangle)",
  "transform": "paste s into d (s.bg -> base); output = tight-crop to template bbox",
  "grid_shape": "tight-crop to template bbox"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task_type: move_to_match (jigsaw-fit into cavity template)
  grid: 15-25 rows x 15-25 cols; bg = most common color
  colors: bg + base + 6-9 sprite colors (all 10 colors used across components)
  template (dst):
    role: rectangular strip-shaped frame made of base color with bg-filled cavity region inside
    base_color: single color, forms largest non-bg connected component
    bbox: tight-crop spans one full dimension of grid (full-width OR full-height strip); touches 3 of 4 grid edges
    bbox_shape: one side equals full grid dim; other side is 7-10 cells thick
    interior: base pixels form an irregular jagged frame; bg-colored hole region fills 39-50% of bbox
    hole_region: single connected (4-conn) mass of bg cells that tiles perfectly into the union of sprite bboxes
    base_connectivity: base cells form 1-2 connected components (8-conn) within template bbox [observed]
  sprites (src):
    location: scattered outside template bbox in the remaining grid area
    count: 7-15 sprites
    separation: each sprite separated from others and from template by >=1 bg cell (distinct 8-conn components)
    bbox: fully filled rectangle (every cell in bbox is non-bg); dims 1x1 up to ~8x6
    palette per sprite: 1 or 2 non-bg colors (never 3+); roughly 70-75% monochromatic, 25-30% bichromatic
    bichromatic_patterns [observed]: checkerboard, dotted grid, or asymmetric two-color split (no fixed pattern required)
    dot_markers: 1-4 sprites are 1x1 of color 0 (black); these also occupy 1 hole cell
    duplicates: some sprites may share bbox dims (puzzle disambiguated by unique color signatures)
  packing_invariant:
    sum(sprite_bbox_area) == hole_region_area (sprites exactly tile the hole)
    each sprite bbox has exactly one axis-aligned placement inside the hole region (non-overlapping perfect tiling)
  output:
    shape: equals template bbox (dh x dw)
    content: base color frame + sprites pasted into their matching cavity positions; no bg or outer-bg cells remain

generation_strategy: output-first, then invert
  1. Sample grid H,W in [15,25]; choose bg, base_color, and 6-9 distinct sprite colors (include 0 for dot markers).
  2. Choose template orientation (vertical or horizontal strip). Pick template bbox (dh, dw) such that one dim equals H or W and the other is 7-10 cells; place it touching 3 grid edges, leaving an L-shaped outer region for sprites.
  3. Build the OUTPUT (template filled): start with (dh,dw) base-colored array. Randomly place 7-15 non-overlapping axis-aligned rectangles that perfectly tile a target-sized (39-50% of dh*dw) connected hole region. Use a recursive guillotine-style or greedy rectangle packing; include 1-4 size-1x1 black (color 0) markers. Ensure hole region stays 4-connected after removal.
  4. Fill each placed rectangle: ~70% monochromatic (pick 1 sprite color), ~30% bichromatic (pick 2 colors with checker/dot/split pattern). Size-1x1 markers always use color 0.
  5. INVERT to get input: create full HxW grid of bg. Paste base-colored template frame into its bbox (the rectangle positions become bg holes again - leave them as bg). For each filled rectangle extracted from step 3, scatter it as a separate sprite in the outer region: find random positions in the L-shaped area outside the template bbox, ensuring each sprite bbox is separated by >=1 bg cell from all others and from template bbox. Paste the sprite with its colors.
  6. Reject if scatter placement fails (outer region too small) or any sprite lands adjacent/overlapping; retry.
```
</generation_spec>
