    color_layers: |
      Top-to-bottom sequence splits into n monochrome groups of sizes 1, 3, 5, ...
      Each group is one color (non-zero); adjacent groups may share or differ in color
    vertical_layout: pixels scattered in upper portion of column with gaps >= 0 between them
    margin: column must have >= n-1 free columns on each side for the pyramid base
  output_pyramids:
    shape: bottom-aligned centered triangle under the source column; row widths 1, 3, 5, ... from top
    each row is monochrome, colored by corresponding layer from input sequence
    all pyramids share the same bottom row (H-1)
  constraints:
    input_fg_rows must all be above the output pyramid rows (no vertical overlap)
    gap between bottommost input pixel and topmost output row >= 0
    when 2 columns present, their pyramid footprints must not overlap horizontally (gap >= 1)
    total foreground pixels conserved between input and output

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid H (16-30), W (9-13), and number of source columns K (1-2).
    2. For each column, pick pyramid height n in {2, 3}. Assign a center column c
       with margin >= n-1 on both sides. If K=2, ensure pyramid footprints don't overlap.
    3. For each column, pick n colors (non-zero, not necessarily distinct) for layers
       1..n. Build the flattened sequence of n^2 pixels (1 of color_1, 3 of color_2, etc.).
    4. Place pyramids bottom-aligned at rows [H-n, H-1] -- this is the output.
    5. For each column, scatter its n^2 pixels into distinct rows of that column,
       all above row H-n (strict). Rows chosen randomly with gaps >= 0; order preserved.
    6. Clear the pyramid region to get the input grid.
